            "heading": "How It Works",
            "text": "Sunlight contains all colors: White light from the sun is actually a mix of every color in the rainbow. Each color travels in waves of different lengths, with red having the longest waves and blue and violet having the shortest waves. Rayleigh scattering: When sunlight reaches Earth, it collides with gases in the atmosphere (mainly nitrogen and oxygen). Because blue light travels in smaller, shorter waves, it bounces around and scatters in every direction much more easily than red or yellow light. Human eye sensitivity: While violet light has an even shorter wavelength than blue light, the sun emits less violet light, and human eyes are much more sensitive to blue light."
          },
